User distribution 
University of Utah, Computer Systems Laboratory
April 10, 1996


     The user collection is a group of programs and libraries that
work with Mach and Lites (and probably UX, but I haven't built them
for UX for a while).  These programs were taken from the USER
collection, release 22 (USER22) distributed by CMU and put into a
``mach4'' style configure framework and some of them were modified to
work with Lites (most notably ps, top and w).  


Why you want the user collection:

     The user collection contains a bunch of programs that are
required to have a fully funtional Mach/Lites system.  Most notably
they include mach_init, which is required to boot.  Other programs
like machid and snames are required for gdb support under Lites.


Programs included in the user distribution:

     Flags, mcreate, swapon, mkill, thstate, envmgr, mnice, top,
     hash_info, mpolicy, vminfo, hostinfo, ms, vmread, ipc_test, mt,
     vmsearch, mach3, netmemoryserver, vmstat, macherr, pinfo, w, machid,
     ps, waitfor, machipc, snames, xptest, massign, stacks, zprint and
     
     mach_init

Libraries included in user:

     libenv, libnetmemory, libservice, libcmucs, libmachid, libnetname and
     libxmm

     (There really isn't much use in having swapon in the collection,
since it dosen't work with mach4 (or mach3 for that matter), but
perhaps someone still needs it.)


INSTALLATION:

Building user22:

     Most of the programs can probably be built with the cross-build
tools, but to really build everything, you'll need to get some sort of
build environment with libc.  This could either be the old CMU mach
libraries/includes or a *BSD/Lites environment.  (For more information
about setting up a *BSD/Lites build environment, take a look at 
http://www.cs.utah.edu/projects/flux/....

     tar xfvz user.tar.gz
     (make an object directory and cd into it)
     ../user/configure --prefix=/usr/mach4
     gmake
     gmake install

     Doing a `gmake install' from the root object directory will
install all the binaries, includes, libraries and manpages for the
user collection.  You'll want to do this as root if you expect ps and
top to work (or you can go back later and make these programs suid
root).

NOTE:

     By default, the programs will install into /usr/mach4 (instead of
the GNU standard /usr/local).  If you want them in /usr/local, then
you'll have to specify that prefix.  The configure script isn't very
good at finding mig if it's not in your path, if it dosen't find it
there, it'll assume that it's in /usr/mach4/bin.


     As usual, if you find any problems, or have enhancements that you
would like to make to them, send mail to either
mach4-users@cs.utah.edu, or mail sclawson@cs.utah.edu directly.  



Stephen Clawson, sclawson@cs.utah.edu
